  "functionalReqs": [
    "Given a long URL, return a unique short URL",
    "Opening the short URL redirects to the original URL",
    "Optional custom alias chosen by the user",
    "Optional expiry time per link",
    "Basic click analytics per link"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Work out how many characters a short code needs: how many URLs will exist over 10 years, and how many codes do 7 characters of [a-zA-Z0-9] give you?",
    "Compare hashing the long URL (then handling collisions) with turning a unique numeric ID into base62. Which avoids collisions entirely?",
    "Use a distributed ID generator (Snowflake or a ticket server with ranges) plus base62, a key-value store keyed by short code, and a cache in front of reads. Choose 301 vs 302 based on whether you need analytics."
  ],
  "level": "Beginner",
  "nonFunctionalReqs": [
    "Redirects in under 50 ms at p99",
    "Highly available: a broken redirect is very visible",
    "Short codes must not be guessable in sequence",
    "Read-heavy: about 100 reads per write"
  ],
  "problemStatement": "Design a service like **bit.ly** or **TinyURL** that turns a long URL into a short alias and redirects anyone who opens the alias to the original URL.\n\nFocus on how short codes are generated without collisions, how redirects stay fast under a heavy read load, and what happens to links over time.",
